WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:01.750
ECONOMY: Hi. If everyone could take this time
to please check their cell phones and turn

00:00:01.750 --> 00:00:02.750
them off, please, so we have no disruption
to our discussion. I wanted to announce this

00:00:02.750 --> 00:00:03.750
meeting will be on the record, and it is being
streamed live on CFR.org and on CFR's YouTube

00:00:03.750 --> 00:00:04.750
channel. Enjoy the discussion. Thank you.

00:00:04.750 --> 00:00:05.750
HAASS: OK. If people could please leave some
hors d'oeuvres for afterwards and we can -- we

00:00:05.750 --> 00:00:06.750
can get going here.

00:00:06.750 --> 00:00:07.750
Seriously, people, we've got quite a few seats
here up front.

00:00:07.750 --> 00:00:08.750
I see 10 over here.

00:00:08.750 --> 00:00:09.750
OK. Good evening. I'm Richard Haass and I
want to personally welcome everybody here

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:14.100
to the Council on Foreign Relations tonight.
Tonight we are here to celebrate, to recognize

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:16.960
and, ultimately, to buy and read.

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:18.980
ECONOMY: Especially buy.

00:00:18.980 --> 00:00:23.800
HAASS: Especially buy, yes. Forced to choose
between buying and reading, we know where

00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:26.080
the authors come out.

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:27.080
(LAUGHTER)

00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:33.500
The title of the book, By All Means Necessary;
the subtitle of the book, How China's Resource

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:41.512
Quest is Changing the World. The authors of
the book, Liz Economy, who is the C.V. Starr

00:00:41.512 --> 00:00:49.059
senior fellow and director for Asia Studies
here at the Council on Foreign Relations.

00:00:49.059 --> 00:00:56.170
And her co-author is Michael Levi. And Michael's
the David M. Rubenstein senior fellow for

00:00:56.170 --> 00:01:02.420
Energy and the Environment, and directs the
program at the Council on energy security

00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:13.490
and climate change. And they are the coauthors
of this new, important and readable book.

00:01:13.490 --> 00:01:22.840
So let's begin, as they sang in that movie,
at the very beginning. Why this title? Why

00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:25.870
-- What does 'By All Means Necessary' refer
to, Liz?

00:01:25.870 --> 00:01:30.450
ECONOMY: OK. Before I get into that, let me
just have my Oscar moment and thank you, Richard...

00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:31.450
(LAUGHTER)

00:01:31.450 --> 00:01:32.450
... for providing...

00:01:32.450 --> 00:01:34.150
HAASS: You want to say how everybody loves
you?

00:01:34.150 --> 00:01:35.150
ECONOMY: OK.

00:01:35.150 --> 00:01:37.270
HAASS: You can have your Shirley McLaine moment
too.

00:01:37.270 --> 00:01:39.060
ECONOMY: I thought it was Sally Field, but
anyway.

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:41.189
HAASS: Sally Field, whatever. Sorry about
that.

00:01:41.189 --> 00:01:46.689
ECONOMY: That's OK. No, but thank you, Richard,
first off for providing such a terrific environment

00:01:46.689 --> 00:01:53.600
in which Mike and I could write the book,
and such patience as well too for us to give

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:55.259
birth to this book.

00:01:55.259 --> 00:02:01.280
And also I think we want to thank our scores
of research associates and interns who provided

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:07.009
us with enormous assistance and, in particular,
our two current research associates, Will

00:02:07.009 --> 00:02:13.590
Piekos and Alex Mahler-Haug. And I think I
want to thank our fifth floor friends as well

00:02:13.590 --> 00:02:16.500
because they really sustain us through all
of this.

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:20.690
HAASS: Just to be clear, fifth floor is their
colleagues, not the mice.

00:02:20.690 --> 00:02:26.510
ECONOMY: Yes. OK. So those are my thanks.
You may have your own thanks to make as well.

00:02:26.510 --> 00:02:31.959
Why don't I take the -- I'll take the title
then. And maybe you want to take the subtitle,

00:02:31.959 --> 00:02:35.660
because they really reflect two different
parts of the book and I think two different

00:02:35.660 --> 00:02:38.010
things that we're trying to get at.

00:02:38.010 --> 00:02:45.370
The title really is a reflection of all of
the tools that China has at its disposal to

00:02:45.370 --> 00:02:49.060
secure resources from outside its boundaries.

00:02:49.060 --> 00:02:53.959
So this can be everything from, you know,
Xi Jinping's personal diplomacy to sort of

00:02:53.959 --> 00:03:00.040
vast trade, aid and investment deals that
we see China attempting to strike when it

00:03:00.040 --> 00:03:03.650
goes to resource-rich countries.

00:03:03.650 --> 00:03:09.230
To the low-cost financing and labor that Chinese
firms can bring to the table, often times

00:03:09.230 --> 00:03:15.580
seeming to undercut Western multinationals.
To other kinds of things like corruption and

00:03:15.580 --> 00:03:21.970
back door deals that can get struck between
some leaders of resource-rich countries and

00:03:21.970 --> 00:03:23.310
their Chinese counterparts.

00:03:23.310 --> 00:03:29.690
It can also mean things like a willingness
to put down stakes, money-losing stakes for

00:03:29.690 --> 00:03:36.620
long periods of time with the hope that a
deal will pay off down the line. So the Chinese

00:03:36.620 --> 00:03:38.709
have a long time horizon in many instances.

00:03:38.709 --> 00:03:39.709
HAASS: Can I ask (inaudible).

00:03:39.709 --> 00:03:40.709
ECONOMY: Yes, OK.

00:03:40.709 --> 00:03:43.110
HAASS: The risk of getting into the substance.
When you say so the Chinese are willing to

00:03:43.110 --> 00:03:47.920
put down stakes and even take a loss for quite
some time in the hope -- and I think your

00:03:47.920 --> 00:03:51.080
words were "the deal will pay off." What does
pay off mean?

00:03:51.080 --> 00:03:58.790
How does -- how do the Chines measure the
value or -- is it by economic? Is it strategic?

00:03:58.790 --> 00:04:01.440
Is it something else? What's their yardstick?

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:03.959
ECONOMY: That's an excellent question.

00:04:03.959 --> 00:04:08.650
Actually, it depends on who you're talking
about. You know, for the Chinese government

00:04:08.650 --> 00:04:13.480
it can mean the strategic sort of element.
So for example, some of you may have just

00:04:13.480 --> 00:04:17.880
seen -- I think it was in the Financial Times
there was an article talking about the National

00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:23.540
Development Reform Commission telling the
Chinese steel producers that they should continue

00:04:23.540 --> 00:04:27.729
to go out and take stakes in iron-ore production,
right.

00:04:27.729 --> 00:04:31.590
But Chinese steel producers have no interest
in that. They have overcapacity. They don't

00:04:31.590 --> 00:04:37.000
want to do that. But the NDRC came out and
said well listen, this is important for strategic

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:41.460
reasons, and because we want to have a say
in global trade. It's important for our voice

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:42.690
in the global trading arena.

00:04:42.690 --> 00:04:46.960
So that's an example of where there's a difference
between the interest of the companies to make

00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:50.730
money and the interest of the government to
kind of have a big political voice.

00:04:50.730 --> 00:04:54.840
HAASS: And I assume when there's a slight
difference of interest it's not always the

00:04:54.840 --> 00:04:55.850
company that wins?

00:04:55.850 --> 00:04:59.940
ECONOMY: No, but it's surprisingly sometimes
the companies have some passive aggressive

00:04:59.940 --> 00:05:01.300
ways of dealing with it.

00:05:01.300 --> 00:05:06.389
HAASS: Michael, before we get much further.
Talk about the subtitle, which is 'How China's

00:05:06.389 --> 00:05:10.780
Resource Quest is Changing the World.' Is
it changing the world, and if so, how and

00:05:10.780 --> 00:05:11.780
how much?

00:05:11.780 --> 00:05:16.080
LEVI: So, first, I want to echo Liz's thanks
to everyone. I won't repeat all of them, but

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:17.080
thank you all to everyone.

00:05:17.080 --> 00:05:19.060
HAASS: You can thank me if you'd like.

00:05:19.060 --> 00:05:21.389
LEVI: Particularly Richard for creating such
a fantastic environment.

00:05:21.389 --> 00:05:25.610
So we picked the subtitle mostly for search
engine optimization.

00:05:25.610 --> 00:05:28.240
ECONOMY: Oh my God. I didn't even know what
that meant.

00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:29.240
(LAUGHTER)

00:05:29.240 --> 00:05:32.410
This is what I get for having Mike as my coauthor.

00:05:32.410 --> 00:05:38.050
LEVI: But secondarily we couldn't call it
"Why China's -- China Resource Quest was Changing

00:05:38.050 --> 00:05:45.950
the World." We're focused on identifying the
different places where China is changing the

00:05:45.950 --> 00:05:47.730
world, but also the places where it isn't.

00:05:47.730 --> 00:05:52.360
So this is not a book about how China's resource
quest is changing everything in the world,

00:05:52.360 --> 00:06:00.050
but about seeing what's real and really what's
myth out there. And I'm not going to list

00:06:00.050 --> 00:06:03.350
all the different ways in which it's changing
the world, but maybe I'll highlight two or

00:06:03.350 --> 00:06:04.990
three.

00:06:04.990 --> 00:06:11.050
There's enormous focus on Chinese investment
in countries. But where the biggest economic

00:06:11.050 --> 00:06:18.500
consequences have come so far is actually
from Chinese trade. Chinese demand for minerals,

00:06:18.500 --> 00:06:23.660
for energy, for food have helped drive up
prices, with huge consequences for economies

00:06:23.660 --> 00:06:27.100
around the world, regardless of whether China
is an investor in those.

00:06:27.100 --> 00:06:32.540
So if you want a headline, top way that China's
resource quest is changing the world, resources

00:06:32.540 --> 00:06:38.740
are more expensive, people want more of them
and the economic consequences for producers

00:06:38.740 --> 00:06:41.220
and for consumers are huge.

00:06:41.220 --> 00:06:46.289
Another really interesting place where China
is changing the world is in its efforts to

00:06:46.289 --> 00:06:52.830
make sure that there are secure routes for
those resources to get back to China. And

00:06:52.830 --> 00:06:57.889
we tend to take this integrated nature of
the global economy for granted that something

00:06:57.889 --> 00:07:01.759
produced in one part of the world can be moved
to another very quickly.

00:07:01.759 --> 00:07:06.139
I don't know that the Chinese do, particularly
when they look at their backyard, at the sea

00:07:06.139 --> 00:07:11.289
lanes near them, congested, contested by a
lot of different countries, for a variety

00:07:11.289 --> 00:07:16.039
of different reasons, not just because of
resource trade. But increasingly I think you

00:07:16.039 --> 00:07:21.410
see China paying attention and asserting its
interests in that region. And we'll see increasing

00:07:21.410 --> 00:07:23.150
security consequences from that.

00:07:23.150 --> 00:07:29.229
HAASS: There's an urban, I don't know if it's
legend or myth or assumption surrounding the

00:07:29.229 --> 00:07:34.960
subject. And the only reason I know it because
I'm an expert on neither China nor Africa.

00:07:34.960 --> 00:07:39.729
Is every time -- many times when I speak publicly
about foreign policy and international relations

00:07:39.729 --> 00:07:45.520
I get asked the question along the following
lines, which says, shouldn't we be worried

00:07:45.520 --> 00:07:51.699
about what China is doing in Africa? Aren't
they cornering the market for some purpose,

00:07:51.699 --> 00:07:56.590
be it financial, strategic or both? And aren't
we going to wake up one day and regret it?

00:07:56.590 --> 00:08:01.569
What is your sense -- is this, if you will,
an urban or nonurban myth? Or is there actually

00:08:01.569 --> 00:08:04.530
something to this? Should we be worried about
China's quest?

00:08:04.530 --> 00:08:10.139
ECONOMY: I'll start and maybe, Mike, you want
to pick up. I think, to Mike's first point,

00:08:10.139 --> 00:08:13.940
one of the interesting things that we found
was that China really -- its resource quest

00:08:13.940 --> 00:08:19.440
primarily rooted in trade rather than investment.
And actually when you look at Chinese investment

00:08:19.440 --> 00:08:23.979
in Africa compared to others, it actually
ranks fourth in the world. It's not the largest

00:08:23.979 --> 00:08:28.259
investor in Africa, which you know everybody
assumes.

00:08:28.259 --> 00:08:34.770
When you look at sort of China's search for
land, you know, it's third, right, after Canada

00:08:34.770 --> 00:08:39.240
and the United States, and quite a distant
third. So it's important to keep everything,

00:08:39.240 --> 00:08:45.980
you know, relative and keep it in proportion
to what actually is going on.

00:08:45.980 --> 00:08:51.420
I think in terms of Africa, certainly it is
exerting a profound impact, you know, in governance

00:08:51.420 --> 00:08:57.970
issues. And so whether you're looking at labor
issues or environmental issues, really just

00:08:57.970 --> 00:09:01.930
have to look back at what China does on the
home front to understand the impact that it's

00:09:01.930 --> 00:09:04.290
having in Africa, as well as other places.

00:09:04.290 --> 00:09:05.290
HAASS: But should...

00:09:05.290 --> 00:09:06.290
ECONOMY: Should we be concerned?

00:09:06.290 --> 00:09:10.140
HAASS: Should we be competing with them more?
Should we basically be talking to African

00:09:10.140 --> 00:09:15.029
governments or others to block China to some
extent? Is this something that we will strategically

00:09:15.029 --> 00:09:16.029
come to rue?

00:09:16.029 --> 00:09:19.910
LEVI: I think it depends on the resource.
And we have to look at the structure of different

00:09:19.910 --> 00:09:24.450
resource markets. And that's one of the reasons
we went so broad in the book.

00:09:24.450 --> 00:09:30.050
If you look at oil, for example, African oil
is still a limited part of the global market.

00:09:30.050 --> 00:09:34.850
The Chinese right now sell most of the oil
they produce in Africa onto the open market.

00:09:34.850 --> 00:09:39.459
Could they in a crisis direct that back to
China? Sure. But there would be plenty of

00:09:39.459 --> 00:09:44.390
other resource from elsewhere to make up for
the suppliers that tend to buy that.

00:09:44.390 --> 00:09:49.279
Now, you can get into other markets where
there's a lot more concentration. And you

00:09:49.279 --> 00:09:54.490
want to make sure there's diversity. So if
China were to go and start to buy up a lot

00:09:54.490 --> 00:09:58.959
of rare earth deposits in Africa, a place
where they have a lot of concentration and

00:09:58.959 --> 00:10:04.470
where there's been a trend toward diversification
that we want to intensify, then that would

00:10:04.470 --> 00:10:09.709
be a place where we have a strong strategic
interest and we would want to divert that.

00:10:09.709 --> 00:10:14.750
And there's another place where it's important,
and this is rooted in economics but gets into

00:10:14.750 --> 00:10:21.700
politics. The Chinese companies can sometimes
make it easy for African governments, Latin

00:10:21.700 --> 00:10:28.860
American governments, other governments to
set up regimes for investment that are not

00:10:28.860 --> 00:10:30.480
particularly strong.

00:10:30.480 --> 00:10:34.440
So there're ones that are appealing to Chinese
companies but don't bring all of the other

00:10:34.440 --> 00:10:39.120
capital and technology and capabilities in.
In the long run that means that we have less

00:10:39.120 --> 00:10:43.510
resources in the world, higher prices for
consumers, less security for all of us. So

00:10:43.510 --> 00:10:48.680
we should be careful to make sure that countries
don't get suckered into doing the easy thing

00:10:48.680 --> 00:10:50.079
that hurts us in the long run.

00:10:50.079 --> 00:10:56.110
HAASS: Liz, I often hear stories that the
Chinese when they invest in Africa often bring

00:10:56.110 --> 00:11:00.930
a lot of their own workforce with them. And
that just like 30, 40 years ago there used

00:11:00.930 --> 00:11:05.770
to be the phrase "the ugly American" for some
of what we were doing in Asia and other parts

00:11:05.770 --> 00:11:10.450
of what used to be the Third World, I actually
hear stories that now the idea of "the ugly

00:11:10.450 --> 00:11:14.230
Chinese" and that in Africa there's lots of
resentment. How true?

00:11:14.230 --> 00:11:20.890
ECONOMY: True. Although primarily in the infrastructure
rather than in areas like mining and oil and

00:11:20.890 --> 00:11:24.700
gas. But still you do find it. And I think
you find it in a couple of different ways.

00:11:24.700 --> 00:11:30.860
So it can be -- and frankly what we're talking
about in terms of Africa applies as well to

00:11:30.860 --> 00:11:35.950
Southeast Asia and sometimes to Latin America.
So this is not -- you know Africa's received

00:11:35.950 --> 00:11:41.320
sort of the bulk of media attention. But really
what Mike and I did was look across the entire

00:11:41.320 --> 00:11:45.470
landscape and find that all these practices
are replicated from one region to the next.

00:11:45.470 --> 00:11:52.500
But to your point, yes ugly Chinese. There's
enormous sort of resentment about -- both

00:11:52.500 --> 00:11:58.510
in terms of when you have Chinese managers
of mines who believe in some ways as some

00:11:58.510 --> 00:12:03.870
foreign ministry officials would say to me
that you know Africans don't work hard, that's

00:12:03.870 --> 00:12:05.500
why we need to bring our labor in.

00:12:05.500 --> 00:12:10.959
They like to sing and dance. They want to
go to church and have labor unions and all

00:12:10.959 --> 00:12:15.950
sorts of inconvenient things, and not work
on the weekends. And so we need to bring in

00:12:15.950 --> 00:12:21.460
our own labor, right. They're willing to work
longer hours and for cheaper rates.

00:12:21.460 --> 00:12:27.060
There's also a problem when it comes to the
individual Chinese going across. Not just

00:12:27.060 --> 00:12:31.110
companies bringing in their management and
then exploiting their labor, but you also

00:12:31.110 --> 00:12:35.769
have, for example, in the case of Ghana you
know several villages in Guangdong province

00:12:35.769 --> 00:12:42.860
simply up and move to Ghana and begin gold
mining and cause a lot of environmental problems.

00:12:42.860 --> 00:12:46.740
So this is not something that's state directed,
and in fact caused enormous problems for the

00:12:46.740 --> 00:12:52.600
foreign ministry and the Chinese government.
In the end, the Ghanaian government kicked

00:12:52.600 --> 00:12:58.510
them out last summer, kicked out 4,500 Chinese
miners. And in retaliation apparently, the

00:12:58.510 --> 00:13:03.280
Chinese have slowed the visa process and are
making it difficult for the Ghanaian government

00:13:03.280 --> 00:13:05.750
to access a $3 billion loan.

00:13:05.750 --> 00:13:13.740
So there is a huge challenge. Issues of communication,
issues of low pay, issues of no labor safety,

00:13:13.740 --> 00:13:19.690
these are all the things that you know mining
workers who work for Chinese mines talk about,

00:13:19.690 --> 00:13:21.980
whether you're talking about Africa or Papua
New Guinea or elsewhere.

00:13:21.980 --> 00:13:26.500
HAASS: Given some of that, if when we're looking
at this as a historical trajectory is it your

00:13:26.500 --> 00:13:30.490
sense that, if you will, the China moment
in Africa has peaked?

00:13:30.490 --> 00:13:34.980
ECONOMY: Well, I don't know whether it's peaked.
I think one of the things that Mike and I

00:13:34.980 --> 00:13:40.010
try to do in the book is not just look at
this very moment, but try to look a little

00:13:40.010 --> 00:13:44.630
bit at what the Chinese are doing to improve
their situation. And so when you're looking

00:13:44.630 --> 00:13:52.230
at issues sort of that are tied into corporate
social responsibility, you do find now that

00:13:52.230 --> 00:13:56.930
you have change being brought. You know in
part by what's taking place at home, right.

00:13:56.930 --> 00:14:01.300
So when you've got the Chinese themselves
demanding better environmental standards from

00:14:01.300 --> 00:14:05.959
their companies that will then become what
the Chinese companies export, or better labor

00:14:05.959 --> 00:14:11.550
standards. You also have pressure from the
outside. States with stronger capacity demand

00:14:11.550 --> 00:14:13.769
that the Chinese do a better job.

00:14:13.769 --> 00:14:19.760
And you have learning. I think in some cases,
for example in Peru, we found you know early

00:14:19.760 --> 00:14:26.399
entrants in the mining sector had very poor
practices across the board. Much later entrants,

00:14:26.399 --> 00:14:31.700
however, began to take advantage of infrastructure
that had developed so that they hired you

00:14:31.700 --> 00:14:37.769
know Western consultants, maybe bring in a
local you know mining head. And so you have

00:14:37.769 --> 00:14:45.050
a much better evaluation of Chinese practices
now, pretty much 10 to 15 years down the line.

00:14:45.050 --> 00:14:49.990
LEVI: Richard, to your question, I don't think
it's peaked rather than the honeymoon is over.

00:14:49.990 --> 00:14:56.050
And people have had experience and they don't
like a lot of that experience.

00:14:56.050 --> 00:14:59.380
And I think it's intensified, and Liz pointed
to that when she talked about Ghana. It's

00:14:59.380 --> 00:15:04.190
intensified as smaller players have been able
to get out there and they're tougher to control.

00:15:04.190 --> 00:15:10.140
When I was working on the book I went to Zambia.
Zambia has this notorious mine, column mine.

00:15:10.140 --> 00:15:16.019
It's a private Chinese mine. Every few months
someone seems to get shot at it, whether it's

00:15:16.019 --> 00:15:18.209
a worker or a Chinese manager.

00:15:18.209 --> 00:15:22.990
And I went and visited with the Chinese ambassador
in Lusaka. And I brought up -- I brought up

00:15:22.990 --> 00:15:27.610
the mine. I was a bit nervous doing it. It's
a very hot button issue. And he said, "If

00:15:27.610 --> 00:15:33.000
you want to buy this mine I will do everything
I can do to have them sell it to you."

00:15:33.000 --> 00:15:34.000
(LAUGHTER)

00:15:34.000 --> 00:15:38.149
It gives you some sense of how uneven the
Chinese government's control is over what's

00:15:38.149 --> 00:15:39.149
happening out there.

00:15:39.149 --> 00:15:41.940
HAASS: I want to come back to that question.
I just got a couple more and then we'll open

00:15:41.940 --> 00:15:46.880
it up to our members and our guests here tonight.
Which is you know the old saw, whatever you

00:15:46.880 --> 00:15:50.290
want to call it, about trade following flag
or flag following the trade.

00:15:50.290 --> 00:15:56.790
What's the balance here? What's the engine?
What's the caboose of this interaction with

00:15:56.790 --> 00:15:57.839
the world?

00:15:57.839 --> 00:16:01.529
ECONOMY: I'm not quite sure. You mean the...

00:16:01.529 --> 00:16:02.529
LEVI: So...

00:16:02.529 --> 00:16:03.529
ECONOMY: I'm not sure...

00:16:03.529 --> 00:16:08.930
LEVI: Is it the companies driving it or is
it the policy driving it? And this is an endless

00:16:08.930 --> 00:16:10.500
question.

00:16:10.500 --> 00:16:15.389
One thing that came up over and over as we
were researching the book is that the Chinese

00:16:15.389 --> 00:16:20.449
government is less capable of directing companies
to do specific things than a lot of people

00:16:20.449 --> 00:16:24.680
believe. They can try to put together packages,
but the companies don't necessarily play ball.

00:16:24.680 --> 00:16:28.360
The CEOs of the biggest companies have equal
rank to the ministers that are supposed to

00:16:28.360 --> 00:16:32.380
govern them. So it makes it tough for one
to win out over the other.

00:16:32.380 --> 00:16:38.630
But what the government does do is create
an environment in which it's attractive to

00:16:38.630 --> 00:16:43.130
a lot of these companies to go out and do
investments in particular ways. They set up

00:16:43.130 --> 00:16:51.760
access to cheap capital. They have a promotion
system, and these leaders are senior party

00:16:51.760 --> 00:16:52.760
members.

00:16:52.760 --> 00:16:56.490
They are not just looking to make a lot of
money, though that's part of it. They're looking

00:16:56.490 --> 00:17:00.941
to get promoted. And promotion means a political
job, a senior political job. And so they're

00:17:00.941 --> 00:17:03.750
going to have the state's objectives in the
back of their mind.

00:17:03.750 --> 00:17:08.189
But it's a more nuanced interaction. It's
really sort of setting the environment in

00:17:08.189 --> 00:17:13.770
the direction rather than dictating the terms
most of the time.

00:17:13.770 --> 00:17:14.770
ECONOMY: Let me just...

00:17:14.770 --> 00:17:15.770
HAASS: Go ahead.

00:17:15.770 --> 00:17:19.289
ECONOMY: Let me just add one thing to just
flesh out what Mike was saying. Which is you

00:17:19.289 --> 00:17:23.360
know if you look, the National Development
Reform Commission will list sort of the key

00:17:23.360 --> 00:17:27.780
resources that they want companies to go out
and get.

00:17:27.780 --> 00:17:32.440
And they will -- the Chinese government will
go out with various ministers, with the heads

00:17:32.440 --> 00:17:36.880
of state-owned enterprises to these countries,
and as Mike said you know structure these

00:17:36.880 --> 00:17:44.039
big deals. And the NDRC will lay out an entire
plan for a country. But companies don't necessarily

00:17:44.039 --> 00:17:45.980
want to participate in those deals.

00:17:45.980 --> 00:17:51.049
So sometimes that's what -- when you read,
for example that you know there's $70 billion

00:17:51.049 --> 00:17:57.780
promised in resource and infrastructure deals
for Brazil dating back since 2007 and only

00:17:57.780 --> 00:18:02.810
30 percent of that has been realized. You
begin to understand that difference between

00:18:02.810 --> 00:18:06.570
what the government is saying and what the
firms are actually doing.

00:18:06.570 --> 00:18:11.940
HAASS: Your subtitle is How China's Resource
Quest is Changing the World. How is China's

00:18:11.940 --> 00:18:16.630
resource quest changing China? What's the
blowback, if you will, from this experience?

00:18:16.630 --> 00:18:22.230
ECONOMY: I think it's -- frankly, I think
it's just beginning. I think there are now

00:18:22.230 --> 00:18:28.530
a couple of different ways in which the Chinese
public has become quite concerned. For example,

00:18:28.530 --> 00:18:34.200
the safety of Chinese citizens, that's something
that the government typically has not paid

00:18:34.200 --> 00:18:35.929
much attention to.

00:18:35.929 --> 00:18:43.230
And in fact, when about 27 I guess workers
from Sinohydro were kidnapped in Sudan back

00:18:43.230 --> 00:18:51.419
in 2012 I want to say, the Chinese government
-- the response form the embassy in Sudan

00:18:51.419 --> 00:18:56.360
was well, you're just going to have to expect
this because as we go out more of our workers

00:18:56.360 --> 00:18:59.050
are going to go out and this kind of thing
is just going to happen.

00:18:59.050 --> 00:19:04.500
And you know the Internet erupted like this
is not acceptable for the Chinese government

00:19:04.500 --> 00:19:08.750
to be saying. And things like you know the
U.S. -- if this happened to the United States

00:19:08.750 --> 00:19:12.590
you know they would send in the Marines and
all this kind of thing. So what is our government

00:19:12.590 --> 00:19:13.890
doing?

00:19:13.890 --> 00:19:19.670
And so I think there's a big sort of upsurge
from the general public concerning this issue

00:19:19.670 --> 00:19:24.730
of personal safety. And now you have Sinohydro,
for example, developing a list of countries

00:19:24.730 --> 00:19:28.120
where they don't want to do business necessarily,
places that they consider dangerous.

00:19:28.120 --> 00:19:32.390
And this is something quite new because that
really does fly in the face of we don't mix

00:19:32.390 --> 00:19:36.190
business with politics. We don't care what's
going on in the internal situation of a country

00:19:36.190 --> 00:19:39.799
-- we're just going there to do business.
But yet the Chinese people and the Chinese

00:19:39.799 --> 00:19:44.450
companies are beginning to say well actually
those situations we don't want to do business.

00:19:44.450 --> 00:19:49.340
I think the other way in which you're starting
to see things change is people are realizing.

00:19:49.340 --> 00:19:53.990
And when you have a change in government like
you have in Burma Myanmar and all of the sudden

00:19:53.990 --> 00:20:00.100
the Chinese you know go from being ostensibly
this trusted, most important partner, to the

00:20:00.100 --> 00:20:06.059
sort of scourge of the region. They're looking
and saying you know obviously we did something

00:20:06.059 --> 00:20:07.059
wrong.

00:20:07.059 --> 00:20:11.490
And so now you have Chinese NGOs traveling
to Burma Myanmar and doing investigations

00:20:11.490 --> 00:20:15.840
and saying to the Chinese companies you've
clearly mishandled this. You know you've -- you

00:20:15.840 --> 00:20:19.790
know all you've done is operate at the top
level. You haven't engaged with the people.

00:20:19.790 --> 00:20:21.830
You need to develop better practices.

00:20:21.830 --> 00:20:26.260
So these are the kinds of changes that we
see taking place. Nascent I would say, but

00:20:26.260 --> 00:20:28.070
this kind of blowback is beginning to affect.

00:20:28.070 --> 00:20:34.669
LEVI: Maybe I'll add one small one onto that.
A big consequence of China's resource quest

00:20:34.669 --> 00:20:40.299
is that the costs of all these resources have
gone way up. And one of the main reactions

00:20:40.299 --> 00:20:45.950
in China is to try and improve efficiency
in order to not have its economy be so exposed

00:20:45.950 --> 00:20:50.230
to this extraordinary cost that it, itself
has helped create. And I think it's a huge

00:20:50.230 --> 00:20:52.039
piece of China's response.

00:20:52.039 --> 00:20:57.540
It's also a big piece that will determine
the future consequences of China's resource

00:20:57.540 --> 00:21:01.990
quest. If they succeed in insulating themselves
from it a bit, they'll also help reduce the

00:21:01.990 --> 00:21:07.440
pressure on the rest of us. And if they fail
to cut their consumption or curb their consumption

00:21:07.440 --> 00:21:10.140
for their own self-interests, we'll all suffer
as a result.

00:21:10.140 --> 00:21:12.490
HAASS: Last question for me then I'll open
it up.

00:21:12.490 --> 00:21:17.900
Do you get the sense with the new leadership
in China there is any reconsideration of this

00:21:17.900 --> 00:21:21.780
policy, given some of the things you've just
alluded to? Are there people now really saying

00:21:21.780 --> 00:21:25.260
are we sure we want to be doing this as much
as we've been doing it, and much in the same

00:21:25.260 --> 00:21:30.380
way as we've been doing it? Is any of this
being, if you will, revisited in a serious

00:21:30.380 --> 00:21:31.380
fashion?

00:21:31.380 --> 00:21:35.560
ECONOMY: I think with -- I haven't seen anything
with just this new leadership. I think that

00:21:35.560 --> 00:21:40.590
there's been an effort, you know within the
ministry of commerce, within some of the bureaucracies

00:21:40.590 --> 00:21:46.630
to start over the past few years beginning
to develop better policies for companies you

00:21:46.630 --> 00:21:49.909
know saying you must undertake an environmental
impact assessment or you're not going to get

00:21:49.909 --> 00:21:50.909
a loan.

00:21:50.909 --> 00:21:55.160
I think that kind of thing has been taking
place. Or you know stock exchanges trying

00:21:55.160 --> 00:22:00.330
to say you know you must report to us if you've
been blacklisted by the Environmental Protection

00:22:00.330 --> 00:22:01.330
Agency.

00:22:01.330 --> 00:22:06.050
I think at that level you do see some things.
I think the foreign ministry's extremely concerned.

00:22:06.050 --> 00:22:13.830
I met with the No. 2 of both the Africa and
the Latin America departments, and they both

00:22:13.830 --> 00:22:19.299
said they're spending an enormous amount of
time setting up like mini consulates out in

00:22:19.299 --> 00:22:26.270
some of these mining areas to deal with all
of the problems that these resource investments

00:22:26.270 --> 00:22:28.820
are causing for the Chinese image.

00:22:28.820 --> 00:22:32.850
So I think that there's consideration at that
level. But I haven't really seen a serious

00:22:32.850 --> 00:22:39.370
rethink yet emanating from the top about the
overall approach to doing investment.

00:22:39.370 --> 00:22:44.200
LEVI: So one place where there's some learning
I think is, at least in some parts of the

00:22:44.200 --> 00:22:48.700
Chinese government there's increasing appreciation
that owning the resources overseas doesn't

00:22:48.700 --> 00:22:53.200
necessarily give you a lot of additional security.
I think there was a very strong belief in

00:22:53.200 --> 00:22:59.490
that direction 10, 15 years ago. There's more
trust of the market, though it's not complete

00:22:59.490 --> 00:23:00.490
yet.

00:23:00.490 --> 00:23:05.990
That said, because they institutionalized
a lot of the incentives that grew out of that

00:23:05.990 --> 00:23:10.659
fear of the mark and the desire to control
resources, the shift in opinions doesn't necessarily

00:23:10.659 --> 00:23:13.410
translate to a shift in how the government
operates.

00:23:13.410 --> 00:23:18.049
I think the other place where they probably
leaned in more or are increasingly leaning

00:23:18.049 --> 00:23:23.809
in more is in the military side. I think this
new leadership, Liz can talk about this more

00:23:23.809 --> 00:23:29.320
than I can, but has really focused on the
military. And that's inevitably going to lead

00:23:29.320 --> 00:23:33.720
to more discussion about China providing its
own security for the sea lanes over which

00:23:33.720 --> 00:23:34.740
its resources travel.

00:23:34.740 --> 00:23:39.440
HAASS: Well, we're not seeing that yet in
the Middle East or Persian Gulf, but I'm curious

00:23:39.440 --> 00:23:40.779
to see it.

00:23:40.779 --> 00:23:41.779
LEVI: Closer to home.

00:23:41.779 --> 00:23:45.190
HAASS: One last question. Sorry. Which is,
did you ever come across anyone in the Chinese

00:23:45.190 --> 00:23:49.450
government who had studied the American or
Japanese experience and said we're going to

00:23:49.450 --> 00:23:51.360
learn from this and do it differently?

00:23:51.360 --> 00:23:52.690
ECONOMY: No.

00:23:52.690 --> 00:23:55.350
HAASS: OK. Just curious.

00:23:55.350 --> 00:23:56.680
ECONOMY: No.

00:23:56.680 --> 00:24:00.340
HAASS: OK. A perfect place to open up -- no
more trick questions.

00:24:00.340 --> 00:24:01.340
(LAUGHTER)

00:24:01.340 --> 00:24:02.340
ECONOMY: I think...

00:24:02.340 --> 00:24:06.550
HAASS: Sure? If people would just introduce
themselves, wait for a microphone. We'll get

00:24:06.550 --> 00:24:07.550
as many...

00:24:07.550 --> 00:24:08.550
QUESTION: Thank you.

00:24:08.550 --> 00:24:09.650
HAASS: ... questions as we can.

00:24:09.650 --> 00:24:13.980
QUESTION: Mahesh Kotacha (ph). Thank you very
much, Elizabeth and your coauthor. Thank you

00:24:13.980 --> 00:24:16.530
for the great introduction to the book.

00:24:16.530 --> 00:24:22.360
My question, Elizabeth, is to you on the issue
of the institutional arrangements with respect

00:24:22.360 --> 00:24:26.630
to infrastructure which the Chinese embed
or try to embed in the countries in which

00:24:26.630 --> 00:24:32.620
they invest into infrastructure. It is said
that there is not as much stress on the rule

00:24:32.620 --> 00:24:39.100
of law and on institutional arrangements for
markets to work to allow prices to work for

00:24:39.100 --> 00:24:44.059
the infrastructure created if it's public-private
type partnerships.

00:24:44.059 --> 00:24:49.640
Could you comment on is that what you meant?
And are they then doing what Richard asked

00:24:49.640 --> 00:24:55.880
about learning from U.S. experience or Western
experience on insisting on rule of law, proper

00:24:55.880 --> 00:25:01.169
contracts and the like in order to invest?
I hear of some kind of efforts on the part

00:25:01.169 --> 00:25:05.730
of the Development Bank of China, China Development
Bank, and the others to try to learn from

00:25:05.730 --> 00:25:08.789
this experience and co-invest in fact?

00:25:08.789 --> 00:25:18.750
ECONOMY: In terms of the infrastructure I
haven't seen much learning taking place. I'm

00:25:18.750 --> 00:25:25.409
trying to think of any cases. Mike I think
probably has looked at the oil and gas sector

00:25:25.409 --> 00:25:30.710
and seen a fair amount of co-investing. But
in terms of -- I haven't seen -- I have to

00:25:30.710 --> 00:25:40.409
say I haven't seen the rule of law become
a particularly important element in the infrastructure

00:25:40.409 --> 00:25:46.789
sector when China's gone abroad to these particular
countries. I haven't seen them pushing that

00:25:46.789 --> 00:25:47.960
issue.

00:25:47.960 --> 00:25:52.870
I think we found more for it to be the case
that states with stronger capacity would push

00:25:52.870 --> 00:25:54.409
the Chinese to do a better job.

00:25:54.409 --> 00:25:59.110
LEVI: And then in oil and gas the Chines often
co-invest out of necessity. They don't have

00:25:59.110 --> 00:26:04.350
the technological capability or the managerial
capability to develop a lot of the big resources

00:26:04.350 --> 00:26:05.350
that are out there.

00:26:05.350 --> 00:26:09.440
But I don't think you see an inclination where
they don't have to work with others to go

00:26:09.440 --> 00:26:10.590
out of their way to do it.

00:26:10.590 --> 00:26:14.690
HAASS: There's one on this side. Sure?

00:26:14.690 --> 00:26:18.460
QUESTION: Greg Lindsay with the World Policy
Institute. I was just curious if you came

00:26:18.460 --> 00:26:22.780
across in your reporting sort of instances
of interesting sort of economic blowback in

00:26:22.780 --> 00:26:27.830
terms of how China is transforming economies
in the sense of where they're locating headquarters,

00:26:27.830 --> 00:26:32.779
locating units, and also how they're using
Africa as a bridgehead to export into it.

00:26:32.779 --> 00:26:36.070
So for example I think one of the things that's
underreported about Dubai is that something

00:26:36.070 --> 00:26:41.110
like 2,000 Chinese companies have set up headquarters
there. There's 150,000 Chinese in Dubai because

00:26:41.110 --> 00:26:45.510
they use it as their North African headquarters,
and because they're exporting so much into

00:26:45.510 --> 00:26:46.590
Africa.

00:26:46.590 --> 00:26:49.570
Considering there's a whole narrative around
the U.S., that we're going to export our way

00:26:49.570 --> 00:26:54.210
into emerging markets as a way to bring back
the American economy, have the Chinese beaten

00:26:54.210 --> 00:26:56.890
us to it in Africa because of that?

00:26:56.890 --> 00:27:04.350
ECONOMY: I think one of the complaints within
Africa is in fact that the Chinese are extracting

00:27:04.350 --> 00:27:09.070
the resources and then exporting finished
products. And one of the things that the Chinese

00:27:09.070 --> 00:27:16.299
have tried to do not only in Africa, but largely
in Africa, is to develop these export zones,

00:27:16.299 --> 00:27:23.799
somewhat modeled on some of the Chinese -- its
own economic development history and trajectory.

00:27:23.799 --> 00:27:29.309
They have not -- although there's a debate
over how effective or successful they've been

00:27:29.309 --> 00:27:34.530
I would say that the weight of evidence is
that they are not particularly successful.

00:27:34.530 --> 00:27:40.850
And you know the idea is to set up manufacturing
zones basically in these countries in order

00:27:40.850 --> 00:27:46.559
to encourage sort of the kinds of skills and
training and development that the African

00:27:46.559 --> 00:27:52.960
countries and others are asking of the Chinese
so that they're not just those neocolonialists.

00:27:52.960 --> 00:27:58.870
And so far I would say we have not heard a
lot of positive reporting about the -- about

00:27:58.870 --> 00:28:04.870
what's been developed. I think maybe there's
one case, Mauritius maybe. But by and large

00:28:04.870 --> 00:28:09.610
you find things like first of all, companies
don't want to participate in them.

00:28:09.610 --> 00:28:14.779
Or for example the -- you know 17 Chinese
companies will be there, but they're all sort

00:28:14.779 --> 00:28:19.150
of subsidiaries of the Chinese construction
companies and they're just really feeding

00:28:19.150 --> 00:28:24.159
into that process and nothing really is developing
that's going to assist in the development

00:28:24.159 --> 00:28:26.010
of the local population.

00:28:26.010 --> 00:28:31.650
So, I think that is the Chinese effort. But
I don't think it's really developed in a way

00:28:31.650 --> 00:28:33.520
that's made much of a difference.

00:28:33.520 --> 00:28:39.320
LEVI: Your point about inward exports into
these countries is interesting. A lot of these

00:28:39.320 --> 00:28:42.890
places when you go to them and you try to
get people to talk to you about what they

00:28:42.890 --> 00:28:46.830
like and they don't like about Chinese activities
and resources, all they want to talk to you

00:28:46.830 --> 00:28:53.360
about is the cheap Chinese goods of the market
that are putting their people out of work.

00:28:53.360 --> 00:28:57.600
And so it's really important when you look
country by country and try to understand the

00:28:57.600 --> 00:29:02.159
impact of this resource quest that you make
sure that the things that you're seeing happening

00:29:02.159 --> 00:29:06.240
are actually a result of that effort to secure
resources. A lot of the times they're about

00:29:06.240 --> 00:29:11.820
these other things, like you point out, that
are tenuously connected to what's happening

00:29:11.820 --> 00:29:13.700
in resources, but really a different phenomenon.

00:29:13.700 --> 00:29:20.040
HAASS: Bob Hormetz (ph)? You need a microphone,
sir.

00:29:20.040 --> 00:29:25.970
QUESTION: Thanks very much. I have a question.
But I just wanted to make one comment on from

00:29:25.970 --> 00:29:29.640
whom the Chinese have learned. The one thing,
if they've learned anything from anyone about

00:29:29.640 --> 00:29:35.570
this, is the European experience, which is,
don't become too dependent on any one supplier.

00:29:35.570 --> 00:29:39.539
I think that's really one of the interesting
things. They saw how the Europeans became

00:29:39.539 --> 00:29:45.159
very heavily dependent on particularly Gazprom.
And they have made a major effort to diversify

00:29:45.159 --> 00:29:47.890
their sourcing, which is rather interesting.

00:29:47.890 --> 00:29:53.973
The point I'm particularly interested in asking
you to pursue a little bit further, there

00:29:53.973 --> 00:29:57.980
are a lot of negatives to the Chinese and
their sort of gold rush to get mines and steel

00:29:57.980 --> 00:30:03.340
and all these other things. But you could
make -- and we've had a lot of issues with

00:30:03.340 --> 00:30:04.340
them.

00:30:04.340 --> 00:30:09.270
The one point you could make, though, on the
other side is to the extent their production

00:30:09.270 --> 00:30:15.169
is additive that they go into countries that
no one else would go into either because their

00:30:15.169 --> 00:30:22.309
social policies are so abhorrent, their politics
are so awful, Sudan and others. Or they simply

00:30:22.309 --> 00:30:25.770
are willing to take these risks that Liz talked
about, which is plunk money down and then

00:30:25.770 --> 00:30:28.789
hope they can get results down the road.

00:30:28.789 --> 00:30:32.659
How do you weigh that, because that really
is an interesting positive-negative yin-yang

00:30:32.659 --> 00:30:35.200
for the markets there?

00:30:35.200 --> 00:30:39.220
ECONOMY: And I'm the negative and Mike's the
positive. So I'm going to let Mike talk about

00:30:39.220 --> 00:30:40.220
this.

00:30:40.220 --> 00:30:41.789
QUESTION: There's obviously a debate and there's
no real answer...

00:30:41.789 --> 00:30:42.789
LEVI: Yes.

00:30:42.789 --> 00:30:43.789
ECONOMY: Yes.

00:30:43.789 --> 00:30:44.789
QUESTION: ... but it's an interesting debate.

00:30:44.789 --> 00:30:45.789
ECONOMY: Right. Yes.

00:30:45.789 --> 00:30:48.720
LEVI: So let me make a very quick observation
on your Gazprom, lesson learned point, which

00:30:48.720 --> 00:30:54.529
is really important. The Chinese have discovered
an alternative to imported natural gas. Unfortunately

00:30:54.529 --> 00:31:00.289
it's coal. It's domestic coal. And it has
-- it creates big problems for them domestically

00:31:00.289 --> 00:31:01.870
and for us internationally.

00:31:01.870 --> 00:31:09.440
But that's one of those places where I'm skeptical
that you'll see enormous Chinese demand for

00:31:09.440 --> 00:31:13.000
importing natural gas and develop -- unless
they can find ways to continually diversify.

00:31:13.000 --> 00:31:15.909
If they can't they'll just choose to use a
different fuel source.

00:31:15.909 --> 00:31:17.130
QUESTION: Or develop their own.

00:31:17.130 --> 00:31:19.630
LEVI: Or develop their own if they can.

00:31:19.630 --> 00:31:25.370
So, on this question of additive versus something
else, yes. I think particularly when the Chinese

00:31:25.370 --> 00:31:29.140
are willing to take risks that others aren't,
that when they are very patient, they don't

00:31:29.140 --> 00:31:34.980
need to report to their investors for the
next quarter, they can have -- they can add

00:31:34.980 --> 00:31:41.580
to the global supply of oil in particular.
So a market that's genuinely global, benefiting

00:31:41.580 --> 00:31:42.820
consumers everywhere.

00:31:42.820 --> 00:31:46.850
I think you have to be a bit careful though.
Again, you can have a bit of a sugar high

00:31:46.850 --> 00:31:52.500
from some of these things with dangerous long-term
consequences. If China's investing in places

00:31:52.500 --> 00:31:58.460
and creating the image of a lot of supply,
that can deter investment in supply elsewhere.

00:31:58.460 --> 00:32:03.080
And so if the Chinese supply ultimately doesn't
materialize or if it's volatile because of

00:32:03.080 --> 00:32:08.000
bad security situations, then you can end
up worse off than when you started. So it's

00:32:08.000 --> 00:32:12.050
a complicated mix. But certainly that force
is there and you have to think about it when

00:32:12.050 --> 00:32:15.240
you're doing a net assessment of what's happening.

00:32:15.240 --> 00:32:17.740
HAASS: Sure?

00:32:17.740 --> 00:32:22.649
QUESTION: Richard Greco from Filangieri Capital
Partners.

00:32:22.649 --> 00:32:28.010
One resource that we can't live without is
water. And my understanding is that China

00:32:28.010 --> 00:32:33.090
has a real shortage and a problem with clean
water for environmental reasons just because

00:32:33.090 --> 00:32:36.890
of a lack of water sources to begin with.

00:32:36.890 --> 00:32:43.400
What's China doing to secure its clean water
sources? Is it succeeding? And if not, what

00:32:43.400 --> 00:32:44.600
are the consequences?

00:32:44.600 --> 00:32:45.919
LEVI: Someone should write a book about that
subject.

00:32:45.919 --> 00:32:49.190
ECONOMY: I know, The River Runs Black.

00:32:49.190 --> 00:32:55.110
OK. So, yes, the water issue. I mean China
is -- primarily China is doing things on the

00:32:55.110 --> 00:33:01.279
home front, right. So whether it's pushing
forward on desalinization or experimenting

00:33:01.279 --> 00:33:07.480
with water pricing, they have a lot of the
sort of traditional tools at their disposal

00:33:07.480 --> 00:33:14.539
that other countries use as well that are
water short, conservation, you know pushing

00:33:14.539 --> 00:33:16.799
forward with a lot of different kinds of experiments.

00:33:16.799 --> 00:33:24.820
In terms of our book, we looked at China's
control of the headwaters of some of Asia's

00:33:24.820 --> 00:33:30.860
most important rivers. And in two cases in
particular we look at the Mekong, we look

00:33:30.860 --> 00:33:37.410
at the Ili, which goes up into Kazakhstan,
and we looked at the Yarlung Zangbo, which

00:33:37.410 --> 00:33:40.559
becomes the Brahmaputra.

00:33:40.559 --> 00:33:46.059
And the Mekong is a different issue because
it's an energy related one primarily, hydropower

00:33:46.059 --> 00:33:52.799
plants. But in both the case of India and
Kazakhstan, there's talk of river diversion,

00:33:52.799 --> 00:33:57.070
right, to help China. And that is actually
something that China does a lot of internally,

00:33:57.070 --> 00:34:01.640
its own river diversions, enormous river diversions.

00:34:01.640 --> 00:34:08.740
And so what we found, and this was a case
where I think the hype surrounding it turned

00:34:08.740 --> 00:34:17.119
out to be greater in the reality. You know
in the case of Kazakhstan the Kazakhs have

00:34:17.119 --> 00:34:23.500
pushed the Chinese. They're worried that the
Chinese have talked about diverting, in several

00:34:23.500 --> 00:34:26.250
places diverting water.

00:34:26.250 --> 00:34:30.149
And the Kazakhs are worried. And they've gotten
the Chinese to the point of talking about

00:34:30.149 --> 00:34:36.300
you know water quality and sort of preliminarily
talking about resource allocation, but no

00:34:36.300 --> 00:34:37.369
formal agreement.

00:34:37.369 --> 00:34:44.030
The Chinese are extremely reluctant to acknowledge
that there are shared water rights in that

00:34:44.030 --> 00:34:49.860
respect. In no case do they recognize that,
and they haven't signed onto the U.N. agreement

00:34:49.860 --> 00:34:55.899
that recognizes that. They're one of only
three countries in the world in fact. And

00:34:55.899 --> 00:34:59.849
so they -- the Kazakhs have made more progress
in many respects than anybody else that we

00:34:59.849 --> 00:35:00.849
looked at.

00:35:00.849 --> 00:35:06.620
In the Indian case certainly there's going
to be damning, more damning of the Yarlung

00:35:06.620 --> 00:35:11.760
Zangbo. But it does not look as though there's
going to be the river diversion aspect of

00:35:11.760 --> 00:35:14.610
it that has received enormous amount of tension
India.

00:35:14.610 --> 00:35:19.710
And some of you may have read Brahma Chellaney's
book on this topic and he's quite concerned.

00:35:19.710 --> 00:35:26.260
He's probably the most vociferous and agitated
person, you know expert scholar out there.

00:35:26.260 --> 00:35:31.630
But for reasons of actual technical feasibility
it doesn't seem possible.

00:35:31.630 --> 00:35:35.660
The Chinese have denied that they have any
such plans on the books. That to me is not

00:35:35.660 --> 00:35:39.710
sufficient proof because lots of times the
Chinese deny things and then quietly start

00:35:39.710 --> 00:35:44.330
doing them. But in this case it seems that
technically it's not even feasible.

00:35:44.330 --> 00:35:47.110
HAASS: Michael Pillsbury (ph)?

00:35:47.110 --> 00:35:55.930
QUESTION: Thank you. I wanted to present you
with a puzzle that I cannot solve. But it

00:35:55.930 --> 00:36:00.070
picks up on both something Michael said about
not trusting the market that they trust the

00:36:00.070 --> 00:36:05.480
market more now but it was a pretty low level
of trust 20 years ago, and something Elizabeth

00:36:05.480 --> 00:36:11.380
started out with about orders from on high,
the National Development Commission and others.

00:36:11.380 --> 00:36:18.930
And we just had about 216 military delegations
come in the Pentagon. I had not been there

00:36:18.930 --> 00:36:25.660
for all 216, but I've been there for most
of them. They often bring up resources and

00:36:25.660 --> 00:36:29.500
they're being slandered for raping Africa
and this kind of thing. They think we do that,

00:36:29.500 --> 00:36:30.869
which we don't.

00:36:30.869 --> 00:36:40.320
But the puzzle is when I've asked them for
books, theories, who back in Beijing is advising

00:36:40.320 --> 00:36:46.310
your leaders about the quest for resources
and all means necessary? Where is this coming

00:36:46.310 --> 00:36:52.370
from? And I thought there'd be no books. Instead
they gave me 20 books over time.

00:36:52.370 --> 00:36:57.579
They've got a formula, this comprehensive
national power formula which everything's

00:36:57.579 --> 00:37:03.390
weighted. Military force is only 10 percent,
resources is much more. They've got complicated

00:37:03.390 --> 00:37:08.910
lists of rich resources to get. As you say,
it's sort of like assignments to the companies

00:37:08.910 --> 00:37:13.960
so that economists know this is stupid. It's
inefficient. You waste money.

00:37:13.960 --> 00:37:18.290
But if -- does it make sense to you? This
is the puzzle. Does it make sense to you that

00:37:18.290 --> 00:37:25.349
their system is still top-down driven enough
that the petroleum university and the people

00:37:25.349 --> 00:37:31.310
who do the CNP scoring are somehow influencing
decisionmakers at the Politbureau level. It's

00:37:31.310 --> 00:37:37.500
driving what Richard asked you, the title
of your book. Go out and do this.

00:37:37.500 --> 00:37:42.400
And when the economists object, Chinese economists,
this is stupid, it's costing us 30 percent

00:37:42.400 --> 00:37:48.910
or 40 percent more than it should, the decision
makers are so certain that we live in a resource,

00:37:48.910 --> 00:37:56.080
almost paranoid resource conflict world, almost
a paranoid view, that these orders must be

00:37:56.080 --> 00:38:00.460
obeyed. And that's what your book essentially
is showing is that the behavior that follows

00:38:00.460 --> 00:38:06.460
from these top-down formulas that are actually
in their reading materials.

00:38:06.460 --> 00:38:10.290
Sorry for the long question, but I wanted
to get the puzzle out to you that I claim

00:38:10.290 --> 00:38:11.460
I can't solve.

00:38:11.460 --> 00:38:16.690
ECONOMY: Do you want to start and then I'll
go?

00:38:16.690 --> 00:38:17.870
LEVI: Sure.

00:38:17.870 --> 00:38:23.220
So, if you're a high-level policymaker, you're
usually not optimizing, you're trying to manage

00:38:23.220 --> 00:38:30.790
risk. And so even if you start to believe
in the market, if you're not totally there

00:38:30.790 --> 00:38:34.890
it might make sense for you to follow some
of this and to still try and encourage your

00:38:34.890 --> 00:38:41.040
companies to invest and to secure resources
in these different ways.

00:38:41.040 --> 00:38:46.000
I think it's only when you start to see some
of the strong countervailing risks on the

00:38:46.000 --> 00:38:51.579
other side, the down sides of doing these
things wrong that you really revisit some

00:38:51.579 --> 00:38:57.530
of that. And so you don't need to only look
to China to see this sort of sense of risk

00:38:57.530 --> 00:39:00.551
aversion among policymakers when it comes
to this. There are plenty of economists in

00:39:00.551 --> 00:39:07.170
the United States who will tell policymakers
that it doesn't matter whether it's a U.S.

00:39:07.170 --> 00:39:10.190
company producing oil abroad or a foreign
company.

00:39:10.190 --> 00:39:14.701
It matters for commercial success and it's
important for American businesses to see it.

00:39:14.701 --> 00:39:21.570
But it doesn't matter for security of oil
supply. But over and over our State Department

00:39:21.570 --> 00:39:26.260
can be persuaded, to different degrees at
different times in our history, but can be

00:39:26.260 --> 00:39:31.210
persuaded to go out and help make sure American
companies succeed in investing in oil, in

00:39:31.210 --> 00:39:33.980
part through an energy security argument.

00:39:33.980 --> 00:39:39.550
So this is a powerful argument. And it turns
out that even hundreds of economists often

00:39:39.550 --> 00:39:42.760
are unsuccessful in persuading policymakers
to change their tactic.

00:39:42.760 --> 00:39:44.500
ECONOMY: Let me just make one...

00:39:44.500 --> 00:39:45.500
HAASS: Quickly.

00:39:45.500 --> 00:39:46.500
ECONOMY: ... small addition.

00:39:46.500 --> 00:39:47.500
HAASS: We got (inaudible) more questions in.

00:39:47.500 --> 00:39:51.460
ECONOMY: OK. So I think one thing that you
certainly appreciate, Mike, is that this has

00:39:51.460 --> 00:39:53.910
a long history within China itself, right.

00:39:53.910 --> 00:39:59.030
And so the idea that the state will direct
the resource quest and will determine even

00:39:59.030 --> 00:40:04.580
within China what resources should be developed
or not developed is -- dates back to the Ming,

00:40:04.580 --> 00:40:09.580
right, when they would say we're not going
to grow tobacco, we're going to grow rice,

00:40:09.580 --> 00:40:16.560
right, or we're going to grow grain because
tobacco is a waste for us. So I think that

00:40:16.560 --> 00:40:23.160
there are long roots to sort of the state-directed
development and quest for resources and control

00:40:23.160 --> 00:40:25.440
over who profits and who participates.

00:40:25.440 --> 00:40:28.750
I also think that the story is probably a
little more complicated than the military

00:40:28.750 --> 00:40:34.232
side is making out because I certainly spoke
to people in the Chinese Academy of Social

00:40:34.232 --> 00:40:42.000
Sciences who talked about how they were pushing
their leaders to be more concerned with resources,

00:40:42.000 --> 00:40:45.530
right, especially when it comes to Southeast
Asia and places like the Philippines and cobalt,

00:40:45.530 --> 00:40:49.770
for example. Please take a more -- take a
more positive approach to the Philippines

00:40:49.770 --> 00:40:54.280
or Vietnam than you're taking because we need
those resources because we get a lot of resources

00:40:54.280 --> 00:40:56.740
from those countries and you're endangering
that.

00:40:56.740 --> 00:41:00.520
So I think that the -- a lot of different
interest groups playing into this at this

00:41:00.520 --> 00:41:01.520
point.

00:41:01.520 --> 00:41:03.800
HAASS: Sergio Galvez (ph).

00:41:03.800 --> 00:41:10.650
QUESTION: Thank you. This goes a little bit
to one of Richard's questions about effects

00:41:10.650 --> 00:41:15.720
within China and within Chinese dynamics.

00:41:15.720 --> 00:41:25.460
A stereotypical view of state-owned enterprises
is that somebody is sitting there deciding

00:41:25.460 --> 00:41:31.099
who pursues what opportunities. And that stereotype
might be right, might not be right, may have

00:41:31.099 --> 00:41:38.910
been right at some point, may not be right
today. But there is anecdotal evidence that

00:41:38.910 --> 00:41:45.550
there's actually an enormous game going on
internally on the pursuit of opportunities

00:41:45.550 --> 00:41:52.460
and competition for the opportunity to pursue
opportunities.

00:41:52.460 --> 00:41:58.290
Can you let us know sort of were you able
to get into that some? And what did you find?

00:41:58.290 --> 00:42:00.690
ECONOMY: Do you want to talk about...

00:42:00.690 --> 00:42:05.869
LEVI: So I mean -- I'm not recalling a specific
instance right here. But there are cases where

00:42:05.869 --> 00:42:10.760
multiple Chinese companies bid against each
other for overseas resources. That doesn't

00:42:10.760 --> 00:42:13.690
sound like a purely state-directed initiative.

00:42:13.690 --> 00:42:19.250
And the reality is these guys are running
big businesses and a lot of this is commercially

00:42:19.250 --> 00:42:24.280
driven. Again, the state creates a framework
within which being commercially successful

00:42:24.280 --> 00:42:29.480
and commercially driven is going to also in
a lot of cases deliver on national goals.

00:42:29.480 --> 00:42:34.700
But that's different from this being all orchestrated
from the top.

00:42:34.700 --> 00:42:40.050
So I think you see that competition. I'm sure
you also see competition politically. Again,

00:42:40.050 --> 00:42:46.240
my guess is that the heads of various oil
companies would all love to be the energy

00:42:46.240 --> 00:42:51.540
minister. Only one of them can be. And it
might be someone else.

00:42:51.540 --> 00:42:55.490
So there's competition to be -- to have a
successful company, to be able to invest,

00:42:55.490 --> 00:42:57.220
but also political competition.

00:42:57.220 --> 00:43:04.680
HAASS: OK. We've got time for -- yes, sir?
Make that the last one. I apologize for the

00:43:04.680 --> 00:43:11.060
several of you we weren't able to get to.
We'll blame it on the authors for the length

00:43:11.060 --> 00:43:12.060
of their answers.

00:43:12.060 --> 00:43:14.350
QUESTION: Thank you. Dick Huber from Invina
Wine Group.

00:43:14.350 --> 00:43:22.550
We export a lot of agricultural products to
China, I mean wine and blueberries and such

00:43:22.550 --> 00:43:31.270
things from Chile and soybeans from Brazil.
And we've always expected to see greater investment

00:43:31.270 --> 00:43:37.140
in agriculture in -- abroad. And we haven't
seen it.

00:43:37.140 --> 00:43:44.440
They -- we love them dearly. They buy. They
tend to pay on time and all those good things.

00:43:44.440 --> 00:43:49.160
But we -- and we'd love to sell them a couple
of vineyards, I mean...

00:43:49.160 --> 00:43:50.160
(LAUGHTER)

00:43:50.160 --> 00:43:56.140
But there's been very, very little investment
in agriculture production, and certainly in

00:43:56.140 --> 00:44:01.640
the three countries I'm involved with, which
are Brazil, Argentina and Chile. You see -- is

00:44:01.640 --> 00:44:05.180
that a barrier? Or is that something just
that will come later? How do you see that?

00:44:05.180 --> 00:44:06.180
Thank you.

00:44:06.180 --> 00:44:09.770
ECONOMY: So I would guess that the wine is
coming. And in fact in the Chinese media there

00:44:09.770 --> 00:44:17.030
was an article just maybe a week ago talking
about the growing interests of Chinese agricultural

00:44:17.030 --> 00:44:20.819
sector in the United States in particular.

00:44:20.819 --> 00:44:27.099
But when I -- I did some work in Brazil for
the book. And when I was there, again thinking

00:44:27.099 --> 00:44:32.980
back to the, you know $70 billion of planned
investment, you know what I heard from Brazilian

00:44:32.980 --> 00:44:39.380
officials was the Chinese delegations would
come one after another to talk about investment.

00:44:39.380 --> 00:44:42.300
But what would happen, a couple of things.

00:44:42.300 --> 00:44:46.859
First, the Chinese expected that the Brazilian
officials could simply you know sign a piece

00:44:46.859 --> 00:44:53.710
of paper and transfer the land to them. That's
not how our land system works here. That may

00:44:53.710 --> 00:44:59.340
be what happens in China, right, officials
and you know business people can collude and

00:44:59.340 --> 00:45:03.800
expropriate land, but that's not how we do
it here. So that was one problem.

00:45:03.800 --> 00:45:06.920
The second one was they said a lot of the
times these Chinese delegations really didn't

00:45:06.920 --> 00:45:12.069
seem that serious about investing. That Brazil
is a nice place to visit.

00:45:12.069 --> 00:45:17.770
All of these you know officials and local
governments have money for foreign trade delegations.

00:45:17.770 --> 00:45:21.880
And so they simply come over, you know spend
a day doing business and then head off to

00:45:21.880 --> 00:45:26.490
Rio and have a good time. So they felt that
there wasn't a lot of seriousness.

00:45:26.490 --> 00:45:30.010
But I do think from talking to the Chinese
side that part of the problem in Brazil in

00:45:30.010 --> 00:45:35.480
particular is a lack of infrastructure as
well. And so there's -- you know and the Chinese

00:45:35.480 --> 00:45:38.510
have tried to give -- and the Brazilians have
tried to give the Chinese interest in that

00:45:38.510 --> 00:45:42.820
part of it and have not been as successful.

00:45:42.820 --> 00:45:48.970
And finally I'll say the Brazilians themselves
were quite worried about potential flood of

00:45:48.970 --> 00:45:54.540
Chinese investment into the agricultural sector
and buying Brazilian land. And so in 2010

00:45:54.540 --> 00:46:01.000
they revised their laws, bringing parts of
the old law back that made it much more difficult

00:46:01.000 --> 00:46:06.240
to own land. We could only do a minority stake
in a joint venture. So in that particular

00:46:06.240 --> 00:46:09.020
case that was a big part of the problem as
well.

00:46:09.020 --> 00:46:11.040
HAASS: Let's just say two things.

00:46:11.040 --> 00:46:15.710
This book on the back flap it says "This is
a Council on Foreign Relations book." And

00:46:15.710 --> 00:46:20.140
it's one of the things we do because books
are an important part of our culture and our

00:46:20.140 --> 00:46:26.740
mission here. And we encourage our fellows,
indeed we expect our fellows to essentially

00:46:26.740 --> 00:46:30.800
make books. It's not the entire part of their
portfolio, but it's a central part because

00:46:30.800 --> 00:46:34.410
in the process of writing a book you've got
to do a depth and breadth of research that

00:46:34.410 --> 00:46:38.590
informs the rest of your work.

00:46:38.590 --> 00:46:46.770
This is but the latest of a book-oriented
culture that we, again, encourage and require

00:46:46.770 --> 00:46:51.730
here. And it's part of our larger commitment
to doing serious policy-relevant scholarship,

00:46:51.730 --> 00:46:58.470
and that's just what this is. This is policy-relevant
scholarship because it deals with this important

00:46:58.470 --> 00:47:04.790
aspect now of China's emergence on the world
stage. And it has consequences for China and

00:47:04.790 --> 00:47:07.839
all the places they're emerging into.

00:47:07.839 --> 00:47:12.790
You have an opportunity to buy the book as
you leave. You will then have an opportunity

00:47:12.790 --> 00:47:19.730
to read the book. But again, the sequence
is important. And at any point in that sequence

00:47:19.730 --> 00:47:25.480
you also have the opportunity to have some
more to eat, to have something to drink, to

00:47:25.480 --> 00:47:31.619
privately ask Liz and Michael some questions,
but most immediately to congratulate them

00:47:31.619 --> 00:47:32.619
on the book they have just published.

00:47:32.619 --> 00:47:33.619
(APPLAUSE)

00:47:33.619 --> 00:47:33.624
ECONOMY: Thanks, Richard. Thank you.

